        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          This graph shows calbindin-28k measurements (ug/mg Protein) in flight and ground control animals from SLS-1. Enzyme-Linked Immunosorbent Assay (ELISA) protocol was used to obtain these measurements.<br/><br/>The results of this study with ELISA of calbindin-D28k did not confirm the reduction in calbindn-D28k found in our earlier study (Sergeev, et al., 1996). Calbindin-D28k levels in kidneys of SLS-1 flight animals after just returning to Earth (R+0), 2.24 +/- 0.17 ug/mg protein, were significantly greater than those of basal controls (L+0), 0.89 +/- 0.06 ug/mg, but not ground controls (GC), 1.97 +/-0.03 (Figure 4). Levels of calbindin-D28k measured by ELISA were equivalent in flight animals immediately following flight (R+0) and after nine days at Earth gravity (R+ML), 1.94 +/- 0.12 ug/mg protein (P=0.5). In SLS-2 animals, renal calbindin-D28k in flight animals after just returning to Earth (R+0), 2.22 +/- 0.27 ug/mg protein, was significantly greater than basal controls (L+0), 1.08 +/- 0.13, (P<0.001) and ground controls, 0.62 +/- 0.04 (P<0.001) (Figure 5). Calbindin-D28k in kidneys of flight animals after return to Earth gravity (R+0) was similar to those of flight animals after 14 days at Earth gravity (R+ML), 2.03 +/- 0.11 (P=0.5) and to animals sacrificed in flight (FD13), 2.75 +/- 0.31 (P=0.06). Thus, the results of this study indicate an increase in calbindin-D28k levels in microgravity. Differences in the results of this and our previous study (Sergeev, et al., 1996) suggest that other factors the animals are exposed to during flight regulate in a substantial way calbindin-D28k. Among these factors may be environmental stresses, such as elevated temperatures (from about 24.5 C to 28.5 C in SLS-2) and reduced humidity (slightly more than 30% to over 100% for SLS-2). Temperature extremes were even greater for SLS-1 (27-32 C).
        </div>

